    image: "closing",
    icon: "M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z",
    lessonsList: [
      {
        id: "negotiation",
        title: "Negotiation Mastery",
        description: "Learn advanced negotiation tactics to maximize deal value.",
        duration: "5 min",
        content: `## Negotiation Mastery
Advanced negotiation is about creating win-win outcomes.
### Key Principles
- Know your walk-away point before you start
- Focus on value, not just price
- Use silence as a powerful tool
- Trade concessions, don't give them away

### Quick Quiz

**Question 1:** What should you know before starting any negotiation?

A) The customer's credit score
B) Your walk-away point
C) The competitor's lowest price
D) The manager's availability

**(Correct: B)** — Knowing your walk-away point prevents you from making a deal that hurts the dealership.

**Question 2:** In win-win negotiation, you should focus on:

A) Price only
B) Value, not just price
C) Beating the customer
D) Getting the highest commission

**(Correct: B)** — Focusing on value keeps the conversation productive and relationship-oriented.

**Question 3:** What is one of the most powerful tools in negotiation?

A) Talking faster
B) Silence
C) Walking away immediately
D) Offering discounts upfront

**(Correct: B)** — Silence creates space for the customer to fill, often revealing their true position.

**Question 4:** When trading concessions, you should:

A) Give them away freely
B) Never make any concessions
C) Trade them — never give them away
D) Only make concessions after the sale

**(Correct: C)** — Every concession should cost the customer something in return, maintaining balance.

**Question 5:** Which negotiation tactic builds the most trust?
A) Starting with an unrealistically high price
B) Being transparent about pricing while emphasizing vehicle value
C) Refusing to negotiate at all
D) Matching any competitor's price sight unseen
**(Correct: B)** — Transparency coupled with value emphasis creates trust while protecting margins.`,
      },
      {
        id: "multiple-decision",
        title: "Multiple Decision Makers",
        description: "Navigate complex sales with multiple stakeholders.",
        duration: "5 min",
        content: `## Multiple Decision Makers
When more than one person is involved, adapt your approach.
### Strategies
- Identify the primary decision maker
- Address each person's concerns individually
- Create a unified vision of value

### Quick Quiz

**Question 1:** When selling to multiple decision makers, your first priority should be:

A) Ignoring everyone except the loudest person
B) Identifying the primary decision maker
C) Making the sale as fast as possible
D) Offering a group discount

**(Correct: B)** — Knowing who makes the final call shapes your entire approach.

**Question 2:** How should you handle each person's concerns?

A) Ignore them and focus on the buyer
B) Address each person's concerns individually
C) Tell them their concerns don't matter
D) Only address the primary decision maker

**(Correct: B)** — Each stakeholder needs to feel heard for the group to reach consensus.

**Question 3:** What should you create for the group?

A) Conflict between members
B) A unified vision of value
C) Separate sales pitches for each person
D) An adversarial negotiation

**(Correct: B)** — A shared understanding of the vehicle's value helps the group move forward together.

**Question 4:** If a spouse disagrees with the primary decision maker, you should:

A) Take the primary decision maker's side
B) Ask clarifying questions to understand both perspectives
C) Ignore the disagreement
D) End the interaction

**(Correct: B)** — Understanding both perspectives helps you bridge the gap and find common ground.

**Question 5:** When presenting to a group that includes a silent decision maker, you should:
A) Ignore the quiet person and focus on the talkative ones
B) Directly invite the quiet person to share their thoughts
C) Assume they agree since they are not objecting
D) Ask the group to have the quiet person leave
**(Correct: B)** — Silent stakeholders often hold the real decision power; inviting their input prevents surprises later.`,
      },
      {
        id: "tough-objections",
        title: "Tough Objections Deep Dive",
        description: "Handle the hardest objections with confidence and skill.",
        duration: "5 min",
        content: `## Tough Objections Deep Dive
Master the art of handling the most challenging objections.
### Common Tough Objections
- "I can get it cheaper online"
- "I'm not sure about this dealership"
- "I've had bad experiences before"
### The Deep Dive Approach
Listen fully, acknowledge their concern, then explore the root cause.

### Quick Quiz

**Question 1:** What is the first step in the Deep Dive Approach?

A) Argue with the customer
B) Listen fully
C) Offer a discount immediately
D) Change the subject

**(Correct: B)** — Listening fully validates the customer and gives you the information needed to respond effectively.

**Question 2:** When a customer says "I can get it cheaper online," you should:

A) Match the online price immediately
B) Acknowledge the concern and explore what else matters to them besides price
C) Tell them online dealers are scams
D) Walk away

**(Correct: B)** — Acknowledging the concern while redirecting to value keeps the conversation productive.

**Question 3:** A customer says "I've had bad experiences before." Your best response is:

A) "That wasn't with us."
B) "I understand. Can you tell me what happened so I can make sure we do better?"
C) "That's not my problem."
D) Ignore it and continue

**(Correct: B)** — Empathy and a commitment to doing better builds trust that can overcome past bad experiences.

**Question 4:** The Deep Dive Approach is effective because it:

A) Forces the customer to admit they're wrong
B) Addresses the root cause of the objection, not just the surface complaint
C) Makes the customer uncomfortable
D) Speeds up the sales process

**(Correct: B)** — Surface objections often mask deeper concerns; addressing the root is more effective than debating the surface.

**Question 5:** A customer says "I had a bad experience at this dealership before." Your BEST response is:
A) "That was under previous management"
B) "I understand, and I'd like to hear what happened so I can make this experience completely different for you"
C) "All dealerships have problems sometimes"
D) Redirect to the vehicle features immediately
**(Correct: B)** — Owning the past and committing to a better present experience rebuilds trust authentically.`,
      },
      {
        id: "referral-systems",
        title: "Referral & Repeat Business Systems",
        description: "Build a system that generates consistent referrals.",
        duration: "5 min",
        content: `## Referral & Repeat Business Systems
Turn every sale into a source of future business.
### The Referral System
- Ask at the right time (when they're happiest)
- Make it easy for them to refer
- Track and follow up on every referral

### Quick Quiz

**Question 1:** When is the best time to ask for a referral?

A) Right after they've signed, when they're happiest
B) When they first walk in
C) After a complaint
D) During price negotiation

**(Correct: A)** — The emotional peak right after purchase is when customers are most likely to enthusiastically refer others.

**Question 2:** How can you make it easy for customers to refer others?

A) Ask them to write a long letter
B) Provide pre-written messages or simple referral cards they can share
C) Require them to bring referrals in person
D) Charge them for the referral program

**(Correct: B)** — Reducing friction increases the likelihood they'll actually follow through.

**Question 3:** What should you do with every referral you receive?

A) Nothing — referrals aren't valuable
B) Track them and follow up consistently
C) Call once and give up
D) Pass them to another salesperson

**(Correct: B)** — Consistent tracking and follow-up turns referrals into a predictable source of repeat business.

**Question 4:** A repeat customer is typically:

A) Harder to sell to than a new customer
B) Easier to sell to and more profitable than a new customer
C) Not worth your time
D) Only interested in discounts

**(Correct: B)** — Repeat customers already trust you, making the sales process faster and more profitable.

**Question 5:** What is the most effective referral incentive to offer a recent buyer?
A) A cash payment
B) A personalized follow-up with genuine appreciation and a simple way to share their experience
C) An aggressive multi-call campaign
D) A mandatory referral quota
**(Correct: B)** — Authentic appreciation and ease of sharing outperform transactional incentives for generating quality referrals.`,
      },
    ],
  },

  // ===== Course 3b: Advanced Closing Part 2 (Plus-gated) =====
  {
    id: "advanced-closing-part2",
    title: "Advanced Closing Strategies Part 2",
    subtitle: "Consultative closing techniques that close more deals",
    description:
      "Master modern consultative closing techniques for today's informed buyers. 4 lessons covering Alternative Choice Close, Assumptive Trial Close, Either/Or Objection Handling, and Micro-Agreements. 7 MCQs per lesson.",
    levels: "Advanced",
    duration: "2 hours",
    lessons: 4,
    image: "closing",
    icon: "M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z",
    lessonsList: [
      {
        id: "alternative-choice",
        title: "The Alternative Choice Close",
        description: "Replace yes/no questions with choice-based closes that assume the sale.",
        duration: "5 min",
        content: "## The Alternative Choice Close\n\nInstead of asking if they are ready to buy, offer two options: Does 60-month or 72-month financing work better? Assume the purchase and focus on logistics.\n\n### Quick Quiz\n\n**Q1:** The Alternative Choice shifts thinking from ____ to ____.\nA) price to value\nB) should I buy to how should I buy\nC) now to later\nD) car to truck\n**(Correct: B)**\n\n**Q2:** When to deploy?\nA) Immediately on greeting\nB) After value is established\nC) Only after they say yes\nD) Never\n**(Correct: B)**\n\n**Q3:** Best Alternative Choice question?\nA) Are you buying this car?\nB) Would you prefer the red or blue?\nC) What color?\nD) Should I call my manager?\n**(Correct: B)**\n\n**Q4:** Why avoid offering one bad option?\nA) It is illegal\nB) Customers see through manipulation\nC) Takes too long\nD) Both are equally good\n**(Correct: B)**\n\n**Q5:** What follows after asking the choice question?\nA) Answer objections immediately\nB) Pause and let them process\nC) Repeat the question\nD) Move to paperwork\n**(Correct: B)**\n\n**Q6:** Which is NOT effective?\nA) 60-month or 72-month?\nB) Friday or Saturday delivery?\nC) Are you SURE you do not want it?\nD) Red exterior or blue?\n**(Correct: C)**\n\n**Q7:** This close works best with:\nA) High pressure\nB) A thorough needs assessment confirming fit\nC) Multiple calls\nD) Immediate discount\n**(Correct: B)**",
      },
      {
        id: "assumptive-trial",
        title: "The Assumptive Trial Close",
        description: "Test commitment right after the test drive before final negotiations.",
        duration: "5 min",
        content: "## The Assumptive Trial Close\n\nRight after the test drive, ask: If we can get the payments where you need them, is this the exact car you want to drive home today? This establishes mental ownership before numbers.\n\n### Quick Quiz\n\n**Q1:** Ideal timing for Assumptive Trial Close?\nA) During greeting\nB) Right after the test drive\nC) After they leave\nD) During paperwork\n**(Correct: B)**\n\n**Q2:** What does this close achieve?\nA) Forces immediate signature\nB) Isolates price as the only remaining variable\nC) Replaces the test drive\nD) Guarantees sale\n**(Correct: B)**\n\n**Q3:** Which phrase plants mental ownership?\nA) If we can get the payments\nB) Is this the exact car\nC) Drive home today\nD) Where you need them\n**(Correct: C)**\n\n**Q4:** If they say not sure, respond with:\nA) Offer a discount\nB) End interaction\nC) What would need to be different?\nD) Repeat the question\n**(Correct: C)**\n\n**Q5:** This close is best described as:\nA) High-pressure tactic\nB) Temperature check before negotiations\nC) Confusion tactic\nD) Replacement for sales process\n**(Correct: B)**\n\n**Q6:** Only use with buyers who already agreed?\nA) True\nB) False\n**(Correct: B)**\n\n**Q7:** After successful trial close, next step:\nA) More trial closes\nB) Send them home\nC) Confidently move to numbers\nD) Start over\n**(Correct: C)**",
      },
      {
        id: "either-or-objections",
        title: "Either/Or Objection Handling",
        description: "Isolate the real objection by framing it as an either/or choice.",
        duration: "5 min",
        content: "## Either/Or Objection Handling\n\nWhen customer says I need to think about it, ask: Is it the trade-in value or the monthly payment? Isolate the real issue and provide a concrete solution.\n\n### Quick Quiz\n\n**Q1:** Primary purpose of Either/Or?\nA) Pressure to buy\nB) Transform vague objection into solvable problem\nC) Confuse customer\nD) End conversation\n**(Correct: B)**\n\n**Q2:** Best Either/Or for I need to think?\nA) Why, what is wrong?\nB) Is it the trade-in or the payment?\nC) OK, call me later\nD) We have other cars\n**(Correct: B)**\n\n**Q3:** What tone to use?\nA) Aggressive\nB) Curious and helpful\nC) Sarcastic\nD) Indifferent\n**(Correct: B)**\n\n**Q4:** Why say I need to think?\nA) Wasting time\nB) Unspoken concern\nC) Already buying elsewhere\nD) Do not like you\n**(Correct: B)**\n\n**Q5:** After identifying real issue:\nA) Celebrate\nB) Provide concrete solution\nC) Ask another Either/Or\nD) Walk away\n**(Correct: B)**\n\n**Q6:** NOT a good Either/Or?\nA) Trade-in or payment?\nB) Color or features?\nC) Why do not you just buy it?\nD) Timing or financing?\n**(Correct: C)**\n\n**Q7:** Best combined with:\nA) Active listening and curiosity\nB) Aggressive negotiation\nC) Ignoring body language\nD) Multiple calls over weeks\n**(Correct: A)**",
      },
      {
        id: "micro-agreements",
        title: "Micro-Agreements",
        description: "Build toward the close with layers of small agreements throughout the sale.",
        duration: "5 min",
        content: "## Micro-Agreements\n\nInstead of one big close, build commitment incrementally. Confirm small agreements: Does this cargo space solve your issue? By paperwork time, they have agreed multiple times.\n\n### Quick Quiz\n\n**Q1:** Core principle of Micro-Agreements?\nA) Repeat same question\nB) Build through layers of small incremental yeses\nC) Use legal jargon\nD) Small discounts throughout\n**(Correct: B)**\n\n**Q2:** What makes it psychologically effective?\nA) People enjoy pressure\nB) People want consistency with prior commitments\nC) People do not notice small questions\nD) People buy on impulse\n**(Correct: B)**\n\n**Q3:** Good micro-agreement during walkaround?\nA) You are buying this, right?\nB) Does this cargo config solve your space issue?\nC) This car is the best, is not it?\nD) Sign here now\n**(Correct: B)**\n\n**Q4:** When NOT to use a micro-agreement?\nA) During needs assessment\nB) During test drive\nC) When customer just raised a genuine concern\nD) During feature demo\n**(Correct: C)**\n\n**Q5:** How many micro-agreements per sale?\nA) One\nB) 4-6, spaced naturally\nC) At least 20\nD) None, just close at end\n**(Correct: B)**\n\n**Q6:** Best post-test-drive question?\nA) On a scale of 1-10, how well does this fit?\nB) You are buying, right?\nC) Did you like it?\nD) Are we done?\n**(Correct: A)**\n\n**Q7:** Micro-agreements eliminate need for final close?\nA) True\nB) False\n**(Correct: B)**",
      },
    ],
  },
